{"records":[{"id":"wpw7mstpp8","title":"Gene sequencing of a rare mutant lung cancer","description":"This sequencing contains 73 genes associated with the occurrence and development of lung cancer, including important genes related to targeted therapy and drug resistance such as BRAF, ALK, RET, ROS1, MET, EGFR, KRAS, ERBB2 in the NCCN guidelines, as well as genes related to PD-1/PD-L1 immunotherapy such as MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2, POLE, and genes related to chemotherapy drug toxicity and efficacy.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-01T15:15:22.885Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wpw7mstpp8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wpw7mstpp8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":" FANGFANG ","last_name":"WANG","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gene Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rtfmf8649f","title":"Gene Expression Profile of Selected Fasciola hepatica Gene Families Implicated in Triclabendazole Resistance","description":"Fasciolosis is caused by liver flukes: F. hepatica, and a sister species – F. gigantica. A growing \nconcern with controlling the disease is resistance to triclabendazole (TCBZ), the only drug \nshown to kill both adult and immature liver flukes. Currently, F. hepatica mechanism of \nresistance to TCBZ is not clearly understood and there is no effective commercially available \nvaccine. Previous work proposed three mechanisms associated with TCBZ mode of action \nand resistance: tubulin binding activity, drug uptake mechanisms, and drug metabolism \nmechanism. Exploring evolutionary forces acting on F. hepatica genes associated with TCBZ \nmode of action and resistance could explain how the parasite develops resistance to the \ndrug, enable identification of potential drug targets, and facilitate development of new \ndrugs.\n\nThe expression profile of the genes associated with TCBZ mode of action was assessed \nacross F. hepatica life stages. Findings indicate that tubulin gene expression was elevated in \nmetacercariae and newly excysted juveniles (NEJs), with a peak expression pattern noticed \nin NEJs 1 hour post excystment, with levels reducing in flukes 21 days post excystment. \nSimilarly, in genes associated with TCBZ uptake, expression was predominantly raised in \nmetacercariae and NEJs, while gene expression gradually reduced towards fluke maturity. \n\nThe effect of TCBZ on F. hepatica was investigated in experimentally infected sheep. \nParasite response to the drug in TCBZ resistant and susceptible F. hepatica isolates was \ncompared in sheep infected and treated with the drug. TCBZ treatment induced gene \nexpression patterns were noticed in 72% (90 out 125 genes, P < 0.05) of all the genes\nassessed (excluding unexpressed genes and constitutively expressed genes). Findings in this \nstudy indicate TCBZ administration affects multiple mechanisms in the parasite. Therefore, \nthis confirms that all the three proposed TCBZ mode of action and resistance mechanisms in \nF. hepatica could be implicated in drug TCBZ resistance.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-21T15:49:05.72Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rtfmf8649f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rtfmf8649f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Olukayode","last_name":"Daramola","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jane","last_name":"Hodgkinson","orcid":"0000-0001-9152-8481"},{"first_name":"Steve","last_name":"Paterson","orcid":"0000-0002-1307-2981"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gene Expression","Fasciola hepatica"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fxcgywft4w","title":"Assessing selective pressure in selected Fasciola hepatica gene families","description":"Fasciolosis is caused by liver flukes: F. hepatica, and a sister species – F. gigantica. A growing concern with controlling the disease is resistance to triclabendazole (TCBZ), the only drug shown to kill both adult and immature liver flukes. Currently, F. hepatica mechanism of resistance to TCBZ is not clearly understood and there is no effective commercially available vaccine. Previous work proposed three mechanisms associated with TCBZ mode of action and resistance: tubulin binding activity, drug uptake mechanisms, and drug metabolism mechanism. Exploring evolutionary forces acting on F. hepatica genes associated with TCBZ mode of action and resistance could explain how the parasite develops resistance to the drug, enable identification of potential drug targets, and facilitate development of new drugs.\n\nF. hepatica gene family members belonging to each of the three proposed mechanism of action of TCBZ action and resistance, and their trematode orthologous sequences were compiled. The gene families studied include tubulins, ATP-binding cassette transporters (ABC), AC, RAS, ADP ribosylation factor, cytochrome P450 (CYP450), GSTs, and Fatty Acid Binding Proteins (FABPs). Signals indicative of positive selection was identified using Phylogenetic Analysis by Maximum Likelihood (PAML) and McDonald and Kreitman test (MKtest). PAML branch-site model testing identified 1 alpha tubulin, 1 delta tubulin, 5 ABC genes, 9 RAS genes, and 4 ADP ribosylation factor genes with statistically significant sites under positive selection. While the MKtest analysis identified 2 RAS genes and 1 AC genes under positive selection.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-21T16:00:25.119Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fxcgywft4w","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fxcgywft4w"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Olukayode","last_name":"Daramola","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jane ","last_name":"Hodgkinson","orcid":"0000-0001-9152-8481"},{"first_name":"Steve","last_name":"Paterson","orcid":"0000-0002-1307-2981"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Evolutionary Biology","Fasciola hepatica"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3x9z4rwdrw","title":"Identification of the resistance mechanism to CDK4/6 inhibition and novel overcoming combination therapy with RNR inhibition for chemo-resistant bladder cancer","description":"The identification of 42 down-regulated protein candidates in treated T24 cells and 46 in RT112 cells compared to the PBS control with 18 overlapping proteins (Supplementary Table S1); At the different time points, 2596, 3674 and 1994 genes were identified as differentially expressed genes (DEGs) (Supplementary Table S2, 3, 4); Identified activation of enormous genes that may confer acquired resistance to CDK4/6 inhibition after 7 days of CDK4/6 inhibition by a CRISPR-dCas9 activation functional genomics analysis, to further narrow down the functional candidates, we applied LFC＞1 and FDR＜0.001 and identified 365 potential candidates in this study (Supplementary Table S5); To further investigate the potential biological interaction network of RRM2 in clinical bladder cancer specimen, we inquired RRM2 in the TCGA cohorts for mRNA co-expression. Through this analysis, we identified the top 10 correlated genes: KIF18B, SKA3, FANCA, MYBL2, CENPA, FAM72B, IQGAP3, E2F1, FAM72D and CDT1 ranked by Spearman's Correlation (Supplementary Table S6); The informaton of prospectively collected 20 MIBC bladder cancer specimens from patients who received radical cystectomy in our hospital. Patients were divided into 3 subgroups according to therapy naïve, chemotherapy responsive and chemotherapy resistance (Supplementary Table S7)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-27T14:50:40.086Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3x9z4rwdrw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3x9z4rwdrw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"智超","last_name":"佟","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ppsfd3wvwk","title":"Fasciola hepatica genome re-annotation and validation of selected gene models","description":"Fasciolosis is caused by liver flukes: F. hepatica, and a sister species – F. gigantica. A growing concern with controlling the disease is resistance to triclabendazole (TCBZ), the only drug shown to kill both adult and immature liver flukes. Currently, F. hepatica mechanism of resistance to TCBZ is not clearly understood and there is no effective commercially available vaccine. Previous work proposed three mechanisms associated with TCBZ mode of action and resistance: tubulin binding activity, drug uptake mechanisms, and drug metabolism mechanism. Exploring evolutionary forces acting on F. hepatica genes associated with TCBZ mode of action and resistance could explain how the parasite develops resistance to the drug, enable identification of potential drug targets, and facilitate development of new \ndrugs.\n\nA re-annotation of the current F. hepatica genome was done using an updated version of \nthe published F. hepatica draft genome (assembly GCA_000947175.1, BioProject PRJEB6687). Subsequently, the current annotation (Fasciola_10x_pilon, GCA_900302435.1 WormBase Parasite Version 15) was compared and critically assessed with the newly re\u0002annotated version. Using coding sequences (CDS) of three well-described annotated gene families, manual validation of the annotation was done. A total of 15,879 F. hepatica genes were identified in this project compared to the 9,401 genes in the current annotation, while differences noticed in both annotations include gene fragmentation, missing exons, and missing genes.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-21T15:50:20.091Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ppsfd3wvwk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ppsfd3wvwk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Olukayode","last_name":"Daramola","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jane","last_name":"Hodgkinson","orcid":"0000-0001-9152-8481"},{"first_name":"Steve","last_name":"Paterson","orcid":"0000-0002-1307-2981"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bioinformatics","Genome Annotation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k66zknby6t","title":"The POLR3A/ARG2/Spermine axis drives malignant progression of tamoxifen-resistant breast cancer ","description":"Tamoxifen resistance was the principal cause for the failure of endocrine therapy in ERα-positive breast cancer. It has been reported that tamoxifen-resistant breast cancer cells exhibit various malignant phenotypes, including enhanced malignant proliferation capacity and anti-apoptotic ability. Meanwhile, arginine metabolic reprogramming has been reported to be associated with the malignant progression of various tumors. In this study, we found the correlation between arginine metabolic reprogramming and tamoxifen resistance in breast cancer by multi-omics analysis including transcriptome and metabolomics. Further studies showed that the key enzyme ARG2 in arginine metabolic pathway was significantly upregulated in tamoxifen-resistant breast cancer cells and was associated with poor prognosis of breast cancer. Functional studies demonstrated that inhibiting the expression or function of ARG2 could markedly suppress the malignant phenotype of tamoxifen-resistant breast cancer cells in both animal and cell models. Molecular mechanism studies further clarified that the transcription factor POLR3A was involved in the transcriptional regulation of ARG2. Overall, this research initially uncovered the role of the POLR3A/ARG2/spermine regulatory axis in tamoxifen resistance of breast cancer, offering novel therapeutic strategies and new drug  targets for tamoxifen-resistant breast cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-02T10:01:02.423Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k66zknby6t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k66zknby6t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lucky","last_name":"ZHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Arginine"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hx5jtyxtxr","title":"Increased lactate secretion by cancer cells sustains non-cell-autonomous adaptive resistance to MET and EGFR targeted therapies. Apicella et al.","description":"Microenvironment is known to influence cancer drug response and sustain resistance to therapies targeting receptor-tyrosine kinases. However if and how tumor microenvironment can be altered during treatment, contributing to resistance onset is not known. Here we show that, under prolonged treatment with tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs), EGFR- or MET-addicted cancer cells displayed a metabolic shift towards increased glycolysis and lactate production. We identified secreted lactate as the key molecule able to instruct Cancer Associated Fibroblasts (CAFs) to produce Hepatocyte Growth Factor (HGF) in a NF-KB dependent manner. Increased HGF, activating MET-dependent signaling in cancer cells, sustained resistance to TKIs. Functional or pharmacological targeting of molecules involved in the lactate axis, such as lactate dehydrogenase or the lactate transporters MCT4 and MCT1, abrogated in vivo resistance, demonstrating the crucial role of this metabolite in the adaptive process. \nThis non-cell-autonomous, adaptive resistance mechanism was observed in NSCLC patients progressed on EGFR TKIs, demonstrating the clinical relevance of our findings and opening novel scenarios in the challenge to drug resistance.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-08-20T07:35:09.484Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hx5jtyxtxr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hx5jtyxtxr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maria","last_name":"Apicella","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Elisa ","last_name":"Giannoni","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stephany","last_name":"Fiore","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karin","last_name":"Ferrari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Fernández-Pérez ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Claudio","last_name":"Isella","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carlotta","last_name":"Granchi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Filippo","last_name":"Minutolo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Antonino","last_name":"Sottile","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paolo","last_name":"Comoglio","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Enzo","last_name":"Medico","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Filippo","last_name":"Pietrantonio","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marco","last_name":"Volante","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Diego","last_name":"Pasini","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paola","last_name":"Chiarugi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Silvia","last_name":"Giordano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Simona ","last_name":"Corso","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mechanism of Resistance","Molecular Mechanism of Cancer","Translational Oncology"],"journal_issn":["15504131"]},{"id":"pcvmnnfd84","title":"Rhododendrol potentiates tetracycline against multidrug-resistant Burkholderia cenocepacia via attenuating virulence","description":"we examined the virulence, pathogenicity, drug resistance, and distribution of specific virulence genes among clinical isolates of B. cenocepacia X85. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-08T18:56:37.786Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pcvmnnfd84","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pcvmnnfd84"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lujun","last_name":"Yin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bacterial Virulence","Antibiotic Resistance"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ty5454trty","title":"Prevalence of Methicillin Resistant Staphylococcus Aureus Among Healthcare Workers in El-Obied city, Sudan ","description":"*The sociodemographic data of the participants \n*Multi drug Resistance pattern of the MRSA isolates ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-12-10T14:02:18.249Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ty5454trty","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ty5454trty"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Altaib","last_name":"Mohemd","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Methicillin-Resistant Staphylococcus aureus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"b232zdtg3m","title":"Isoorientin data","description":"Research Data of the  manuscript \"Isoorientin reverses lung cancer drug resistance by promoting ferroptosis via the SIRT6/Nrf2/GPX4 signaling pathway\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-13T14:57:00.662Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/b232zdtg3m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/b232zdtg3m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"senling","last_name":"feng","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Reference Material","Database"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":122849,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":154,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122849,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":122369,"restricted":60,"embargoed":420},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2075},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1329},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":565},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":448},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":417},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":387},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nív